      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Critics like Bowles & Gintis (1976) and Bowles (1979) argue that standardized testing serves as a tool of social control, reinforcing existing inequalities by facilitating the recruitment of able-bodied students into elite educational institutions. By controlling access to higher education, these institutions perpetuate privilege by funneling more resources and opportunities to students from the middle and upper classes. Moreover, critics like esteemed sociologist Tom Popkewitz (1989) suggest that the testing industry itself is a key driver of educational inequality, as standardized tests create a multibillion-dollar market for standardized test preparation and tutoring services, which disproportionately target low-income communities, further exacerbating the wealth gap.",
